<PlainLayout title="Networks — Pilot Protocol (plain)" description="Private networks in Pilot Protocol - group-level connectivity, membership permissions, join rules, and the security model." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/docs/networks/">

<p><a href="/plain/docs/">&larr; Docs index</a></p>

<h1>Networks</h1>

<p>Networks are a group-level access control primitive in Pilot Protocol. A network grants connectivity to all its members at once, removing the need for agents to establish trust with each other individually.</p>

<h2>Overview</h2>
<p>Adding multiple agents to the same network allows them to discover and connect to each other. The network boundary serves as the trust boundary. Networks are managed through the `pilotctl network` commands. Private Network is in early access.</p>

<h2>Networks vs. bilateral trust</h2>
<p>Pilot Protocol has two access control models: bilateral trust and network membership. They can be used together.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Scope: Bilateral trust is one-to-one. Network membership is group-wide.</li>
  <li>Setup: Bilateral trust requires a handshake and approval for each pair. Network membership requires adding an agent once to connect to all members.</li>
  <li>Scaling: For n agents, bilateral trust requires O(n²) handshakes. Network membership requires O(n) additions.</li>
  <li>Discovery: With bilateral trust, an agent's address or hostname must be known. Network members can be enumerated with `pilotctl network members`.</li>
  <li>Revocation: Bilateral trust is revoked per-peer. Network access is revoked for a member from the entire network at once.</li>
  <li>Use case: Bilateral trust is for cross-organizational collaboration or unknown peers. Networks are for agents within the same organization, like teams or fleets.</li>
  <li>Persistence: Bilateral trust is stored locally on each node. Network membership is stored in the registry and persists through node restarts.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What network membership grants</h2>
<p>Agents that share a network gain a specific set of permissions.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Discover addresses: Members can resolve the endpoints of other members, even if they are private.</li>
  <li>Open connections: Connection attempts from other members are accepted.</li>
  <li>Send datagrams: Datagrams from other members are delivered.</li>
  <li>List members: A full list of members is available via `pilotctl network members`.</li>
  <li>Handshake auto-approval: Network membership acts as a signal for auto-approving trust handshakes.</li>
</ul>
<p>Network membership does not grant:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Traffic inspection: Encryption is end-to-end between agents. The network provides connectivity, not visibility.</li>
  <li>Transitive access: If agent A and B share network 1, and B and C share network 2, A cannot reach C. Each network is an independent trust domain.</li>
</ul>
<p>Enterprise networks add additional controls. An enterprise network can be created with `pilotctl network create --name prod --enterprise`. Features include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>RBAC: Three-tier roles (owner, admin, member).</li>
  <li>Invites: A consent-based invite flow.</li>
  <li>Identity &amp; SSO: OIDC, SAML, Entra ID, LDAP, and webhook identity providers.</li>
  <li>Directory sync: Push entries from AD/Entra ID/LDAP to provision members.</li>
  <li>Policies: Membership caps, port whitelists, and network descriptions.</li>
  <li>Audit &amp; compliance: Structured audit events and SIEM export.</li>
  <li>Blueprints: Declarative JSON provisioning.</li>
</ul>

<h2>The backbone (network 0)</h2>
<p>Every registered agent belongs to network 0, the backbone. This is the global address space. The backbone provides:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Address allocation: 32-bit node IDs assigned at registration.</li>
  <li>Endpoint resolution: Public IP:port discovery via STUN.</li>
  <li>NAT traversal: Hole-punching and relay coordination.</li>
  <li>Handshake relay: Trust negotiation via the registry.</li>
</ul>
<p>The backbone does not grant communication rights. A private agent's endpoint is withheld from agents outside its trusted peers and network co-members. Other directory metadata like hostname and tags may still be resolvable.</p>

<h2>Join rules</h2>
<p>A join rule is set during network creation with `pilotctl network create` to control how new members are added.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Open: Any node can join without approval.</li>
  <li>Invite only: An owner or admin must invite new members.</li>
  <li>Token-gated: Anyone with a shared secret token can join.</li>
</ul>
<p>For token-gated networks, agents can self-join with the token:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network join 1 --token my-secret</code></pre>

<h2>Network lifecycle</h2>
<p>To create a network:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network create --name research-lab --join-rule token --token my-secret</code></pre>
<p>The `--join-rule` can be `open`, `invite`, or `token`. The `--enterprise` flag enables enterprise features.</p>
<p>Admins add agents by an identifier:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Node ID (e.g., `1001`) from `pilotctl info`.</li>
  <li>Pilot address (e.g., `1:0001.0000.03E9`) from daemon startup output.</li>
  <li>Hostname (e.g., `my-agent`) set with `--hostname`.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>pilotctl network invite 1 1001
# or by hostname / pilot address
pilotctl network invite 1 my-agent
pilotctl network invite 1 1:0001.0000.03E9</code></pre>
<p>To monitor agents, list live members with `pilotctl network members &lt;network_id&gt;`. This shows node ID, hostname, version, and public status. JSON output includes address, real_addr, and last_seen.</p>
<p>To remove agents:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl network kick 1 1001
# or by hostname / pilot address
pilotctl network kick 1 my-agent</code></pre>
<p>Access is revoked immediately. To delete a network, owners can run `pilotctl network delete &lt;network_id&gt;`. This removes all member associations but preserves agents' backbone registration and bilateral trust relationships.</p>

<h2>How it works under the hood</h2>
<p>Network membership is checked automatically at three points in the protocol.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Address resolution: When resolving an agent's endpoint, the registry checks if the agent is public. If not, it checks for a shared network or mutual trust. If neither exists, the endpoint lookup is denied. Directory metadata may still be returned.</li>
  <li>Connection acceptance: When a connection request arrives at a private agent, the daemon checks if the source is on its trust list or a member of a shared network. If not, the request is silently dropped.</li>
  <li>Datagram delivery: Datagrams sent to private agents are checked the same way as connections. If the sender is not trusted or in a shared network, the datagram is silently dropped.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Security model</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Membership is the boundary: In standard networks, membership grants access. Enterprise networks add RBAC roles and port-level policies.</li>
  <li>Silent rejection: Connection attempts to a private network agent from a non-member are silently dropped, preventing scanning.</li>
  <li>Backbone isolation: Membership in the backbone (network 0) does not grant communication rights. A private agent's endpoint is hidden from those outside its trust and network boundaries.</li>
  <li>Immediate revocation: `pilotctl network kick` revokes access immediately. The registry is the single source of truth.</li>
  <li>No transitive trust: Network membership is not transitive. Each network is an independent trust domain.</li>
  <li>Enterprise port policies: Enterprise networks support port-level policies to restrict access. Use `pilotctl network policy &lt;network_id&gt; --allowed-ports 80,443,1001` to set allowed ports. Other flags include `--max-members` and `--description`.</li>
</ul>

